    vitals: "HR: 91 bpm, BP: 103/68 mmHg, RR: 25 breaths/min, Temp: 38.6°C, SpO2: 88%",
    labs: "No routine labs. If impetigo suspected: swab for culture (Staph aureus common). IgE: elevated (not diagnostic).",
    imaging: "No imaging indicated.",
    diagnosis: "Acute Moderate-Severe Atopic Dermatitis Flare with Secondary Impetiginisation (Staph aureus superinfection)",
    ddx: ["Atopic Dermatitis", "Contact Dermatitis", "Seborrhoeic Dermatitis", "Scabies", "Psoriasis"],
    goals: "Reduce inflammation and pruritus, treat secondary infection, restore skin barrier, prevent flares",
    pharm: "1. Topical corticosteroids (step-up potency for flare): face: Hydrocortisone 1% BID for 5-7 days. Body: Betamethasone Valerate 0.1% cream BID for 7-10 days, then step down to Hydrocortisone. 2. Topical calcineurin inhibitor (for sensitive areas): Tacrolimus 0.03% ointment BID (face, flexures) as steroid-sparing. 3. Emollients: Apply thick emollient (50/50 white soft paraffin/liquid paraffin or Cetomacrogol) LIBERALLY and FREQUENTLY (every 2-4 hours when awake). 4. Antihistamine: Cetirizine syrup 5mg BID or Hydroxyzine 10-25mg at bedtime (for itch and sleep). 5. Antibiotics for impetigo: Flucloxacillin 250mg QID (child dose 12.5 mg/kg QID) for 7 days. 6. Wet wrap therapy if severe (short-term). 7. Avoid triggers: soap, bubble bath, wool, overheating.",
    nonPharm: "Bleach baths (dilute, 1/4 cup household bleach in full bathtub) twice weekly to reduce Staph colonisation. Cotton clothing. Maintain cool environment. Short, lukewarm baths (5-10 min) with emollient wash, pat dry, then apply topical within 3 minutes.",
    carePlan: "Start potent topical steroid to body for 7 days, mild to face. Start oral Flucloxacillin for impetigo. Start antihistamine. Increase emollient use. Review in 1 week. If improved, step down to lower potency steroid. Educate mother on \"treating the flare and preventing the next.\"",
    dtps: "1. Acute moderate-severe atopic dermatitis flare with bacterial superinfection. 2. Chronic disease requiring long-term maintenance therapy and trigger avoidance.",
    monitoring: "EASI score weekly. Assess for signs of infection (weeping, honey-coloured crusts, fever). Monitor growth (children with severe atopic dermatitis may have growth delay if topical steroids overused or disease severe). Sleep quality.",
    counselling: "Eczema is a chronic condition — treatment involves both treating flares and maintaining healthy skin between flares. Moisturisers are the most important daily treatment — apply at least 3-4 times daily. Steroids are for flares only, use as directed. Stop scratching by keeping nails short and using antihistamines at night.",
    followUp: "Dermatology clinic in 1 week. If recurrent infections: consider bleach baths 2x/week. If severe and persistent: consider referral for systemic therapy (Dupilumab, Cyclosporine, or Methotrexate).",
    pearls: "The eczema treatment ladder: emollients (daily) → topical steroids + calcineurin inhibitors (for flares) → phototherapy → systemic immunomodulators. Emollients are the cornerstone of management — they reduce steroid requirements and prevent flares. There is NO role for antihistamines in eczema management except for sleep disturbance from itch (they do not reduce histamine-mediated itch in eczema). Secondary staphylococcal infection is common — treat promptly with anti-staphylococcal antibiotics. Topical steroids should be used: apply to active eczema, use the weakest effective potency, stop when clear, restart when flares.",
    references: ["NICE Guidelines: Atopic Eczema", "AAAAI Guidelines: Atopic Dermatitis", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Dermatology"],
