    pharm: "1. Systemic therapy initiation — Methotrexate (first-line): Loading dose 7.5mg once weekly PO/IM, escalate to 10-15mg weekly based on response (max 25mg). Folic acid 5mg weekly (taken on a different day to methotrexate — e.g., methotrexate Monday, folic acid Friday). Pre-treatment: FBC, LFTs, Cr, Hep B/C, HIV, TB screen, chest X-ray. 2. Monitoring on methotrexate: FBC and LFTs every 2 weeks for first month, then monthly for 3 months, then every 2-3 months. Cr every 3 months. 3. If methotrexate fails or is contraindicated: consider Biologic therapy (TNF inhibitors: Adalimumab, Etanercept). TB screening mandatory before TNF inhibitor (Mantoux + chest X-ray, consider QuantiFERON if available). 4. Continue topical therapy as adjunct: Betamethasone Valerate 0.1% ointment to plaques OD for 2 weeks, then weekends only. Calcipotriol/Betamethasone combination to scalp daily for 4 weeks, then weekends. 5. Scalp-specific: Coal tar + salicylic acid shampoo (Capasal) or coal tar solution (Exorex). 6. Emollients: liberal application BID (Cetomacrogol, white soft paraffin/liquid paraffin).",
    nonPharm: "Weight loss (obesity worsens psoriasis). Smoking cessation (increases risk of psoriasis and treatment failure). Limit alcohol (interferes with methotrexate hepatotoxicity). Stress reduction. UV light exposure (moderate sun exposure can improve psoriasis). Moisturise daily. Avoid skin trauma (Koebner phenomenon).",
    carePlan: "Initiate Methotrexate 7.5mg once weekly. Folic acid 5mg weekly (different day). Baseline bloods done. Review bloods at 2 weeks. If tolerated and no significant LFT/FBC derangement, escalate to 10mg at week 4. Review PASI at 12 weeks. If PASI 75 achieved, continue. If partial response at 16 weeks, escalate to 15mg. If methotrexate fails or contraindicated, refer for biologic therapy. Continue topical treatments for scalp and focal plaques.",
    dtps: "1. Moderate-severe plaque psoriasis failing topical therapy — systemic therapy indicated. 2. Methotrexate is first-line systemic agent: effective, affordable, well-established. 3. Must exclude hepatitis, TB, and HIV before starting immunosuppression.",
    monitoring: "FBC and LFTs at 2, 4, 8, 12 weeks, then every 2-3 months. Cr every 3 months. Monitor for: bone marrow suppression (sore throat, bruising, fever — stop methotrexate and check FBC immediately), hepatotoxicity (LFTs >3x normal — hold), pneumonitis (dry cough, dyspnoea — chest X-ray). Avoid in pregnancy (teratogenic — contraception mandatory). Avoid excessive alcohol.",
    counselling: "Methotrexate is a once-weekly tablet — NEVER take it daily (fatal overdose risk). Take folic acid on a different day to reduce side effects (mouth ulcers, nausea, hair thinning). Blood tests are essential to check for side effects on your liver and blood counts. You must use reliable contraception during treatment and for 3 months after stopping. Avoid alcohol or limit to minimal intake. Report any sore throat, fever, or unusual bleeding immediately — these could indicate low blood counts.",
    followUp: "Dermatology clinic in 4 weeks for bloods and dose escalation. PASI assessment at 12 weeks. If biologic therapy needed: dermatology referral and TB screening. Annual cardiovascular risk assessment (psoriasis is an independent CV risk factor). Screen for psoriatic arthritis at each visit (joint pain, morning stiffness, enthesitis).",
    pearls: "Methotrexate is the most commonly used systemic agent for moderate-severe psoriasis — PASI 75 response rate ~40-50%. The mechanism involves dihydrofolate reductase inhibition, reducing skin cell proliferation. Folic acid supplementation does NOT reduce efficacy but significantly reduces side effects (mucositis, nausea, hepatotoxicity). TB reactivation is a risk with TNF inhibitors — always screen before initiating. In Kenya, methotrexate is available through KEMSA and is the most affordable systemic option. Biologics (Adalimumab biosimilar) are increasingly available but cost remains a barrier.",
    references: ["BAD Guidelines: Psoriasis Management", "NICE Guidelines: Psoriasis", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Dermatology", "WHO: Global Report on Psoriasis"],
