    goals: "Eradicate dermatophyte infection, prevent spread to contacts, restore hair growth, prevent complications (kerion, scarring alopecia)",
    pharm: "1. Oral antifungal therapy (topical agents alone are ineffective for tinea capitis — infection involves the hair follicle): Griseofulvin 10-20 mg/kg/day (or 125-250mg TID for child weighing 20-30kg) for 6-8 weeks. Take with fatty food or milk to enhance absorption. 2. Alternative: Terbinafine 62.5-125mg daily for 4-6 weeks (equally effective for Trichophyton species; preferred if available). 3. adjunctive: Selenium sulfide 2.5% shampoo or Ketoconazole 2% shampoo twice weekly to reduce shedding and transmission. 4. If kerion (inflammatory, boggy mass): consider adding short-course oral corticosteroids and extending antifungal course. 5. Treat all affected household contacts and close contacts (school screening may be needed). 6. Topical antifungal alone (e.g., clotrimazole) is NOT sufficient — must use oral therapy.",
    nonPharm: "Keep hair short for easier treatment application. Wash towels and combs in hot water. Avoid sharing combs, hats, and pillows. Treat household contacts concurrently if affected. Regular shampooing to reduce scale.",
    carePlan: "Start Griseofulvin 125mg TID (or Terbinafine 62.5mg OD if weight <20kg, 125mg OD if >20kg) for 6-8 weeks. Prescribe Selenium sulfide shampoo 2x/week. Advise mother to wash child's bedding and towels separately. Screen household contacts. Review at 4 weeks for clinical improvement and adherence. If no improvement, consider culture and species identification, switch to terbinafine if initially on griseofulvin (or vice versa).",
    dtps: "1. Tinea capitis requires systemic antifungal therapy — topical agents cannot penetrate the hair follicle. 2. School-age children are the most affected demographic. 3. Treatment adherence over 6-8 weeks is critical for cure and prevention of recurrence.",
    monitoring: "Clinical improvement (reduced scaling, hair regrowth) expected at 2-4 weeks. Full course completion is essential — stopping early leads to recurrence. Hair regrowth may take 2-3 months after treatment completion. Monitor for side effects: Griseofulvin — headache, GI upset, photosensitivity, rare blood dyscrasias. Terbinafine — GI upset, rare hepatotoxicity. KOH confirmation recommended before starting treatment.",
    counselling: "Complete the full 6-8 weeks of medicine even if the patches look better before then. The medicine must be taken with food or milk. Wash your child's combs, hats, and pillowcases in hot water every week. Tell the school so other children can be checked. Do not share combs, brushes, or hats. Your child can return to school once treatment has started for 48 hours.",
    followUp: "Dermatology or paediatric clinic in 4 weeks. If no improvement at 4 weeks, reconsider diagnosis and refer for culture. Hair regrowth: 2-3 months post-treatment. School health follow-up for contact screening.",
    pearls: "Tinea capitis is the most common dermatophyte infection in children worldwide. In sub-Saharan Africa, Trichophyton soudanense and Trichophyton tonsurans are the predominant species — neither fluoresces under Wood's lamp (unlike Microsporum species). Griseofulvin has been the traditional treatment for decades, but terbinafine is now considered equally effective for Trichophyton species and has a shorter course (4-6 vs 6-8 weeks). Topical therapy alone fails because the fungus resides within the hair shaft. The \"black dot\" pattern (endothrix infection) is characteristic of T. tonsurans. Post-inflammatory alopecia may persist for months after successful treatment but hair usually regrows.",
